1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,413
The second attempt began with a test the group had not designed.

2
00:00:03,413 --> 00:00:14,937
A member of the college council, a guy named Wen, handed them a list of five buildings and said that a student leader should walk the route with his eyes closed.

3
00:00:14,937 --> 00:00:18,577
He was joking; nobody laughed.

4
00:00:18,577 --> 00:00:26,112
Beneath the joke lay a fair demand: the map had to work for someone who had never seen the campus.

5
00:00:26,112 --> 00:00:36,972
Hence the rule that Lin, usually the fastest walker, would go first, while the president of the council waited at the gate.

6
00:00:36,972 --> 00:00:39,802
Initially the walk went well.

7
00:00:39,802 --> 00:00:47,701
Then he lost track of the alley behind the sports hall, where a showy artificial sign pointed nowhere.

8
00:00:47,701 --> 00:00:52,116
Both of them stood there, and her patience narrowed.

9
00:00:52,116 --> 00:00:56,306
“Normally a map is a promise,” she said.

10
00:00:56,306 --> 00:01:08,921
“This one is a luxury.” Elsewhere, water from the swimming pool had accumulated along the avenue, and the ugly cloth banner above the door was too wet to read.

11
00:01:08,921 --> 00:01:15,851
A small crowd began to gather behind them, which was worse than the rain.

12
00:01:15,851 --> 00:01:19,826
They resolved to test the wording instead of the route.

13
00:01:19,826 --> 00:01:27,226
On behalf of the volunteers, Maya rewrote each line and duly recorded every hesitation.

14
00:01:27,226 --> 00:01:33,125
“Preferable” became “better”; “atop the hill” became “at the top of the hill”.

15
00:01:33,125 --> 00:01:47,175
Gradually the sentences grew shorter, their eyesight of the real problem sharpened, and the emergence of a telling detail came on the fourth page: nobody had described the door from the outside.

16
00:01:47,175 --> 00:01:53,655
“We should often read our own report as a stranger would,” she said.

17
00:01:53,655 --> 00:01:57,610
The practicality of the project troubled Lin.

18
00:01:57,610 --> 00:02:04,900
To cultivate trust, he thought, you must first sow patience and then reap attention.

19
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:16,459
A blouse soaked by rain, a cap left idly on a bench, and a troublesome locked gate all told the same story: the map was accurate and unusable.

20
00:02:16,459 --> 00:02:26,599
Ironically, the second walk missed the gate narrowly, and the narrowest point of the route was the one the designer had photographed most carefully.

21
00:02:26,599 --> 00:02:33,664
A major question had been answered, and it had produced a worse answer than doubt.

22
00:02:33,664 --> 00:02:38,274
The reunification of the two maps took three evenings.

23
00:02:38,274 --> 00:02:50,408
It was no luxurious achievement and nobody wanted to celebrate it, but it was an advancement: the group could develop one gate at a time and conquer it in a single pass.

24
00:02:50,408 --> 00:02:55,143
The number of wrong turns had fallen, though not far.

25
00:02:55,143 --> 00:03:01,973
The smell near the canteen began to sicken Lin, and the queue continued to grow in the rain.

26
00:03:01,973 --> 00:03:06,213
“We have not overcome the city,” Maya said.

27
00:03:06,213 --> 00:03:10,648
“We have only stopped lying about it.”
